WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.869
Infrastructure-as-Code automation security
is a layered engineering practice.

2
00:00:07.949 --> 00:00:12.581
Start by protecting repositories and
review paths.

3
00:00:12.661 --> 00:00:19.925
Use pull requests, branch protection, code
ownership, protected environments,

4
00:00:20.005 --> 00:00:26.847
and review requirements to ensure
infrastructure changes are intentional.

5
00:00:26.927 --> 00:00:34.296
Separate low-trust triggers from
privileged execution so untrusted events do not

6
00:00:34.376 --> 00:00:38.798
receive the authority to change
infrastructure.

7
00:00:38.878 --> 00:00:42.668
Scope automation identities and secrets.

8
00:00:42.748 --> 00:00:45.801
Plan, validate, apply, rollback,

9
00:00:45.881 --> 00:00:51.144
and administrative tasks may need
different permissions.

10
00:00:51.224 --> 00:00:57.330
Use approved secret delivery or platform
identity where practical.

11
00:00:57.410 --> 00:01:03.937
Protect state files with access control,
encryption where appropriate,

12
00:01:04.017 --> 00:01:07.701
locking, versioning, and auditability.

13
00:01:07.781 --> 00:01:14.624
Review plans before high-impact applies so
risky replacements, deletions,

14
00:01:14.704 --> 00:01:19.652
exposure changes, and permission changes
are visible.

15
00:01:19.732 --> 00:01:26.153
Trust modules, providers, templates, and
dependencies deliberately.

16
00:01:26.233 --> 00:01:33.181
Review ownership, provenance, defaults,
versioning, and update practices.

17
00:01:33.261 --> 00:01:40.104
Use policy-as-code, validation, secure
defaults, metadata requirements,

18
00:01:40.184 --> 00:01:47.448
and environment-specific rules to catch
problems before they reach production.

19
00:01:47.528 --> 00:01:54.897
Document and approve exceptions so they
remain visible rather than becoming quiet

20
00:01:54.977 --> 00:01:55.924
bypasses.

21
00:01:56.004 --> 00:02:03.373
Finally, separate environments,
credentials, state, variables, and approvals.

22
00:02:03.453 --> 00:02:07.769
Promote changes through reviewable
workflows.

23
00:02:07.849 --> 00:02:15.113
Monitor what automation does, keep useful
evidence, plan rollback and recovery,

24
00:02:15.193 --> 00:02:22.773
and feed incidents, drift, failed runs,
and exceptions back into better guardrails.

25
00:02:22.853 --> 00:02:28.643
The goal is infrastructure change that is
controlled, reviewed,

26
00:02:28.723 --> 00:02:35.460
least-privileged, observable, recoverable,
and continuously improved.
